    "readable_text": "Item 1 \u2014 Tutorial: Introduction\n\nContent:\nWhen two logarithmic terms in an expression have the same base and argument, we can combine them by adding their coefficients.\n\nContent:\nTo demonstrate, let's look at the expression\n[MATH: 5\\log_{\\color{blue}4}{\\color{red}7}+3\\log_{\\color{blue}4}{\\color{red}7}.]\n\nContent:\nBoth logarithmic terms have a base of [MATH: \\color{blue}4] and an argument of [MATH: {\\color{red}7}.] Therefore, we can combine them by adding their coefficients:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: 5\\log_{4} 7 + 3\\log_{4} 7|= (5 + 3)\\log_{4} 7 = 8\\log_{4} 7]\n\n---\n\nItem 2 \u2014 Example: Simplifying Logarithmic Expressions Containing Two Terms\n\nExample Prompt:\nSimplify [MATH: 2\\log_{2}{5}+4\\log_{2}{5}.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nBoth logarithmic terms have a base of [MATH: 2] and an argument of [MATH: 5.] Therefore, we can combine them by adding their coefficients:\n\n[MATH: 2\\log_{2} 5 + 4\\log_{2} 5|= (2 + 4)\\log_{2} 5 = 6\\log_{2} 5]\n\n---\n\nItem 3 \u2014 Question 1\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: \u2212 2\\log 3 + 3\\log 3 =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 3\\log 3]\nb. [MATH: 5\\log 3]\nc. [MATH: 2\\log 3]\nd. [MATH: \\log 3]\ne. [MATH: \u2212 \\log 3]\n\n---\n\nItem 4 \u2014 Question 2\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: 3\\ln 5 \u2212 \\ln 5 =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: \u2212 3\\ln 25]\nb. [MATH: 2\\ln 5]\nc. [MATH: \u2212 3\\ln 5]\nd. [MATH: 2\\ln 10]\ne. [MATH: \\ln 10]\n\n---\n\nItem 5 \u2014 Example: Simplifying Logarithmic Expressions Containing More Than Two Terms\n\nExample Prompt:\nSimplify [MATH: 2\\log_4(9)-3\\log_3(9)+5\\log_4(9).]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nBoth the first and the third logarithmic terms have a base of [MATH: {\\color{blue}4}] and an argument of [MATH: {\\color{red}9}.] Therefore, we can combine them by adding their coefficients:\n\n[MATH: 2\\log_{4} (9) \u2212 3\\log_{3} (9) + 5\\log_{4} (9)|= (2 + 5)\\log_{4} (9) \u2212 3\\log_{3} (9); = 7\\log_{4} (9) \u2212 3\\log_{3} (9)]\n\n---\n\nItem 6 \u2014 Question 3\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: \u2212 9\\log (20) + 3\\log (20) + 2\\log (10) =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: \u2212 27\\log (40) + 2\\log (10)]\nb. [MATH: \u2212 6\\log (20) + 2\\log (10)]\nc. [MATH: \u2212 9\\log (20) + 5\\log (10)]\nd. [MATH: 3\\log (20) \u2212 7\\log (10)]\ne. [MATH: \u2212 4\\log (20)]\n\n---\n\nItem 7 \u2014 Question 4\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: 7\\log_{5} (2) \u2212 5\\log_{5} (2) + 6\\log_{5} (2) =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 18\\log_{5} (2)]\nb. [MATH: 8\\log_{5} (2)]\nc. [MATH: 18\\log_{5} (10)]\nd. [MATH: 7\\log_{5} (2)]\ne. [MATH: 8\\log_{5} (10)]\n\n---\n\nItem 8 \u2014 Example: Simplifying Logarithmic Expressions Containing Variable Expressions\n\nExample Prompt:\nSimplify [MATH: 9\\log_6(2x)-2\\log_4(2x)-3\\log_4(2x).]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nBoth the second and the third logarithmic terms have a base of [MATH: {\\color{blue}4}] and an argument of [MATH: {\\color{red}2x}.] Therefore, we can combine them by adding their coefficients:\n\n[MATH: 9\\log_{6} (2x) \u2212 2\\log_{4} (2x) \u2212 3\\log_{4} (2x)|= 9\\log_{6} (2x) + (\u2212 2 \u2212 3)\\log_{4} (2x); = 9\\log_{6} (2x) \u2212 5\\log_{4} (2x)]\n\n---\n\nItem 9 \u2014 Question 5\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: 2\\log (3x) + \\log (6x) \u2212 2\\log (6x) =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: \\log (3x)]\nb. [MATH: 2\\log (3x) \u2212 2\\log (9x^{2})]\nc. [MATH: 2\\log (3x) \u2212 \\log (6x)]\nd. [MATH: 0]\ne. [MATH: 2\\log (3x) \u2212 2\\log (6x)]\n\n---\n\nItem 10 \u2014 Question 6\n\nPrompt:\nWhich of the following is equivalent to [MATH: 3\\ln sqrt(x) \u2212 12\\ln sqrt(x)?]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 9\\ln (x)]\nb. [MATH: \u2212 4\\ln sqrt(x)]\nc. [MATH: \u2212 9\\ln sqrt(x)]\nd. [MATH: 9\\ln sqrt(x)]\ne. [MATH: 36\\ln (x)]"
